## Engineering outcome — 5 October 2026

The [course engineering update](https://hq.mega.dev/challenges/what-it-shows#engineering-update)
now records #4471 as closed on 5 October:

- [Parser issue #1](https://github.com/Abilyne/mega-s01e03/issues/1): **fixed** — `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`. Closed as completed.
- [Hint issue #2](https://github.com/Abilyne/mega-s01e03/issues/2): **won’t fix** — booking-v3 form retired. Closed as not planned; no hint fix was shipped.

These are the desk’s reported engineering outcomes. The original export predates them.
Our first closure treated both as completed analysis and missed this new evidence; this
update corrects that distinction. The report counts below remain historical. The five
unusual observations still lack individual reproduction or resolution evidence in the
provided material; the parser closure does not prove each was explained.

## Finding and scope

The [original incident](/kb/incidents/4471/) calls this one known parser issue. Its rendered
cause table contradicts that claim: booking-v3's form hint is a separate problem without
its own ticket. The shared parser fix did not fix that hint; retiring the affected form removed that path.

This review uses the desk export dated 4 October 2026. It is a historical snapshot, not a
live queue. All totals below are computed from unique report IDs merged into this ticket,
joined to the tenant directory. Browser locale and intake tags are not ground truth.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## Distinct causes and validation

**Parser:** the shared parser treats dates as month-first even for day-first tenants.
Use the tenant's date convention at that boundary, preferably converting validated inputs
to an unambiguous date representation. Test app, email, and booking-v2 paths, ambiguous
dates such as 07/04, dates whose day exceeds twelve, and month-first tenants for regressions.

**Hint:** booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY`, while its form reads dates in the day-first
tenant's convention. Make the displayed hint and accepted format agree. Test the form
for Alder Physio, Copper Studio, and Pale Harbour; changing the shared parser alone
cannot correct misleading instructions on this path.

<Reassessment />

## What the queue shows

Reports arrived before the July merge wave. The wave records desk consolidation, not
the beginning of the incident. Reports continue after consolidation, through the end
of the export; a known label and a queued fix are not evidence of recovery.

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

## Stop merging on subject alone

The [triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe/) and [accepted decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject/)
are stale. Their prose says matching subjects establish a duplicate, but their own
interactive examples distinguish causes by tenant and entry channel. Identical wording
can hide different causes; different wording can describe the same cause. An unknown
tenant does not establish a match.

<DedupeExplorer />

Keep decision 0001 unchanged as historical evidence. Proposed replacement: read the
report, identify tenant locale and form version, inspect the entry path and observed
behavior, then merge only when the cause is supported. Keep uncertain reports visible
for investigation. The desk owner must adopt this policy and review new arrivals against
the split; this exported page does not change the live desk's workflow.

## Follow-up

The two linked engineering issues now record different closure reasons. Preserve report IDs and
the exceptional observations when moving reports. After each fix, replay representative
cases and monitor fresh reports by cause, not by subject or the old ticket's status.
Refresh this page from a new export before presenting its counts as current.

